    "body": "## Overview\n\nFixes the shared-prefix junction under per-group EAGLE flags (#61455): with a dropping EAGLE group in the lookup, siblings that share a prefix get 0 prefix-cache hits under sparse retention (default `prefix_cache_retention_interval=0`). The junction becomes a set of positions, `{J - A, J}`, computed in one place (`junction_boundaries()`) and used by every junction consumer. This is an alternative to #62206, which keeps an extra SWA tail at `J - block_size` inside the SWA mask only.\n\nThe MooncakeStore side (store lookup as a junction source, `store_mask` as a consumer) is a follow-up PR stacked on this one: #62400.\n\n## Claims\n\n- DeepSeek-V4-Flash + MTP (retention 0): siblings that diverge 8 tokens past an aligned shared prefix of 3840 tokens get 0 hit tokens on main and 3584 with this PR. Siblings that diverge 160 tokens past it get 3840 on both.\n- Shared-prefix GSM8K (1319 questions): the prefix-cache hit rate goes from 4.7% to 82.1%, and accuracy does not change (0.943 \u2192 0.945).\n- One helper produces the junction; all consumers read it: SWA and Mamba retention masks, the Mamba junction checkpoint, the scheduler's chunk stops (one per position) and `OffloadingConnector`.\n- No behavior change without EAGLE, or when no dropping (non-Mamba) EAGLE group takes part in the lookup.\n\n## Validation\n\n**End to end** (GB200, 4 GPUs, base 83c9a547bf):\n\n```\nvllm serve deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash --tensor-parallel-size 4 --kv-cache-dtype fp8 --block-size 256 \\\n  --enable-prefix-caching --enable-prompt-tokens-details \\\n  --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"mtp\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":2}'\n```\n\nJunction probe: requests A, B, C, D share exactly F + d token ids (F = 3840 = 15 \u00d7 256), then each has its own 1500-token suffix. They are sent one at a time; the table shows `cached_tokens` of C and D.\n\n| d | main | this PR |\n|---|---|---|\n| 8 | 0, 0 | 3584, 3584 |\n| 160 | 3840, 3840 | 3840, 3840 |\n\nShared-prefix GSM8K: every prompt is the same 3848-token prefix (instructions, 8 train exemplars, filler), then about 600 tokens of per-question distractor text, then the test question. 1319 questions, concurrency 32, greedy, `max_tokens` 512.\n\n| | main | this PR |\n|---|---|---|\n| accuracy | 0.943 | 0.945 |\n| prefix-cache hit rate (`vllm:prefix_cache_hits / queries`) | 4.7% | 82.1% |\n| `cached_tokens` per question | 0 for 1248 questions | 3584 for 1247, 3840 for 71 |\n\nThe per-question suffix is longer than the hit alignment on purpose. With a shorter suffix, a request's own prompt-end replay tail lands on the shared prefix and hides the bug.\n\n**Unit tests** (CPU):\n\n```\npytest -n 16 tests/v1/core/test_prefix_caching.py tests/v1/core/test_single_type_kv_cache_manager.py \\\n  tests/v1/core/test_kv_cache_utils.py tests/v1/core/prefix_cache tests/v1/core/test_scheduler.py \\\n  tests/v1/kv_connector/unit/offloading_connector tests/v1/kv_connector/unit/test_mooncake_store_*.py \\\n  --deselect tests/v1/core/test_prefix_caching.py::test_hybrid_prefix_cache_hit_rate \\\n  --deselect tests/v1/core/prefix_cache/test_hybrid_prefix_cache_hit_rate.py\n1695 passed\n```\n\n`test_swa_shared_prefix_reuse_under_zero_retention_eagle` covers uniform and mixed block sizes, with only the SWA draft group flagged and with all groups flagged. `test_mamba_shared_prefix_reuse_eagle_draft_group` covers a Mamba hybrid with a flagged draft group. The offloading scheduler test checks that every junction position is a reachable boundary. The flagged cases fail on main.\n\n## Details\n\n**Root cause.** The junction is where the longest single-group hit exceeds the reconciled hit. A dropping EAGLE group needs a peek block past its hit. So after #61455, a sibling that shares F tokens hits F in a non-EAGLE group but at most F \u2212 A in the dropping group (A = hit alignment). Main pinned only F, and no sibling can resume there.\n\n**Why a set.** Where a sibling resumes depends on how far past F it diverges. Divergence of less than the drop unit past F resumes at F \u2212 A; divergence further than that resumes at F. When the drop unit is smaller than A (DeepSeek-V4: SWA block 64, A 256), both cases occur, so a single position cannot serve both. The junction is therefore `{F \u2212 A, F}` whenever a dropping EAGLE group takes part in the lookup.\n\n**Why not #62206.** #62206 fixes the SWA mask only. The Mamba checkpoint, the scheduler chunk stop and `OffloadingConnector` keep the wrong junction.\n\n**Supersedes the first version of this PR.** The first version moved the junction to `F \u2212 A` only. That lost the hit at F for siblings that diverge further than the drop unit (a regression with mixed block sizes). This version keeps both positions.\n\n**Cost.** One extra retained tail per junction, only when a dropping EAGLE group exists. Without EAGLE, the junction is one position, as on main.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR. Claude Code wrote and verified the change and ran the GPU checks; a Kimi Code agent fleet reviewed the design and wrote test extensions; Codex reviewed the diff.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
